WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.607
Course structure is the path learners follow through the experience.

00:00:03.607 --> 00:00:05.361
Menus, sections, lessons, slides, blocks,

00:00:05.361 --> 00:00:10.270
and navigation patterns should help learners understand where they are and what comes next.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:13.076
A good structure creates a sense of progress.

00:00:13.076 --> 00:00:17.285
It also makes the course easier to maintain because related content is

00:00:17.285 --> 00:00:20.090
grouped logically instead of scattered across disconnected screens.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.000
Learning flow should move from context to explanation to example to practice or reflection.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.806
Not every module needs all of those pieces,

00:00:27.806 --> 00:00:32.014
but learners usually benefit from knowing why a topic matters before they

00:00:32.014 --> 00:00:33.768
are asked to remember details.

00:00:33.768 --> 00:00:35.521
Progressive disclosure is useful here.

00:00:35.521 --> 00:00:37.625
Instead of showing everything at once,

00:00:37.625 --> 00:00:41.834
reveal information in manageable layers when the learner is ready for it.

00:00:41.834 --> 00:00:44.289
Screen layout matters because it shapes attention.

00:00:44.289 --> 00:00:48.848
Visual hierarchy tells learners what to read first, what supports the main point,

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:50.601
and what action to take.

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:54.810
Use headings, spacing, contrast, grouping, and consistent patterns to guide the eye.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.317
A screen should not make every element compete for attention.

00:00:58.317 --> 00:01:00.772
When everything is emphasized, nothing is emphasized.

00:01:00.772 --> 00:01:01.824
Avoid overcrowded screens.

00:01:01.824 --> 00:01:04.279
Dense paragraphs, too many icons, excessive motion,

00:01:04.279 --> 00:01:07.085
and unnecessary decorative elements can increase cognitive load.

00:01:07.085 --> 00:01:11.644
A strong screen supports learning by making the main idea easier to process.

00:01:11.644 --> 00:01:16.203
Decoration is only useful when it clarifies meaning, sets context, or improves navigation.

00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:19.008
The goal is not to fill a canvas;

00:01:19.008 --> 00:01:22.165
the goal is to make the learning task easier.

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:26.373
One practical screen-design test is to ask what the learner should notice

00:01:26.373 --> 00:01:28.127
within the first few seconds.

00:01:28.127 --> 00:01:32.335
If the answer is unclear, the screen may need a stronger heading,

00:01:32.335 --> 00:01:34.790
fewer competing elements, or a simpler layout.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:39.700
The best screens feel calm because the designer has already decided what matters most.
